Hands-On Lab 1: Simulating Readiness Failures

Let’s deploy an application where we can manually toggle health to watch readinessProbe cut off traffic without killing the pod.

1. Create the Manifest (probes-demo.yaml)

cat <<EOF > probes-demo.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: probe-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: probe-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: probe-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /healthz
            port: 80
          initialDelaySeconds: 2
          periodSeconds: 3
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /
            port: 80
          initialDelaySeconds: 5
          periodSeconds: 5
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: probe-service
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: probe-app
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
EOF

kubectl apply -f probes-demo.yaml


2. Observe the Initial State

Check the pods immediately:

kubectl get pods -l app=probe-app

Notice: Under READY, you will see 0/1. Why? Because Nginx returns a 404 Not Found for /healthz out of the box! The container is Running, but the readiness probe fails.

Check your endpoints:

kubectl get endpoints probe-service

Notice: The ENDPOINTS column will be completely empty (<none>). Kubernetes refuses to send traffic to an unready pod.

3. Make One Pod Healthy

Let's create the /healthz file inside only one of the running pods:

FIRST_POD=$(kubectl get pods -l app=probe-app -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")

# Create the health endpoint file inside nginx web root
kubectl exec $FIRST_POD -- touch /usr/share/nginx/html/healthz

4. Check Endpoints Again

kubectl get pods -l app=probe-app
kubectl get endpoints probe-service

Verification:

The first pod will transition to 1/1 READY.

The second pod remains 0/1 READY.

kubectl get endpoints probe-service will now list only the IP of the healthy pod!

Run these steps in your Codespaces terminal, and verify the output of kubectl get endpoints probe-service.
